<p>
  I grew up in Estarreja, Portugal, and it's still the place I call home. I like being able to open
  the door and see woodland and dirt tracks, knowing that town is half an hour's walk away and I can
  go fishing whenever I feel like it. It's a small place where people know each other, and much of
  my life still revolves around the people and activities here.
</p>

<h2 id="childhood">Taking things apart</h2>

<p>
  As a child, I took apart remote-controlled cars, old computers and pretty much anything I could
  get my hands on. During the summer holidays when I was ten, I decided to do the same to my
  mother's computer, the only one we had at home, while she was at work. After dismantling and
  cleaning the whole thing, I had to work out how to put it back together without being able to look
  up a YouTube tutorial: the only computer I could use was now in pieces, and I'd never done this
  before. I was connecting the last front-panel wire as she was parking the car.
</p>
<p>
  Around that time, I was starting to find reasons to build things too. I love birds, pretty much
  anything with a beak and feathers, so when my grandfather accidentally left their enclosure open
  and my favourite dove escaped, I was devastated. She eventually came back, but I didn't want to go
  through that again. At about eleven, I fitted a battery-powered buzzer that would sound for as
  long as the door was open.
</p>
<p>
  My grandfather had a lot to do with my interest in electrical work, and I also wanted to become an
  electrician. Electronics was a difficult hobby for a child with no money, though: components were
  expensive, and ordering them online wasn't straightforward. I ended up spending more time
  programming, where I could experiment and write my own programs even if my computer was a potato.
</p>

<h2 id="work">Hardware, software and a soldering iron</h2>

<p>
  I went on to study Computer and Informatics Engineering at the University of Aveiro, finishing my
  bachelor's in 2024. I'm still there, now doing a master's in Computer and Telematics Engineering,
  and I still enjoy bringing code and electronics together. Spending an afternoon soldering and
  finally getting everything to work is one of my favourite parts of it.
</p>

<p>
  Of the projects I've built at university, the{" "}{
