    ) : (
      "AES IoT gateway"
    )
  }{" "}are among my favourites because they let me work on both the hardware and the software, as
  did the{" "}{
    projectLinks.aditus ? (
      <a href={projectLinks.aditus}>Aditus access system</a>
    ) : (
      "Aditus access system"
    )
  }. Sometimes wanting to try something gives me more work than the project actually needs: in an
  unpublished experiment with one of the weather-station sensors, I used an ATtiny to communicate
  with the ESP32 over I²C, even though the ESP32 had plenty of spare pins. I wanted to build my own
  I²C peripheral and find out how it was done.
</p>
<p>
  When one of those experiments doesn't work, I separate the problems and deal with them one at a
  time until I find the fault. There are still plenty of things I want to explore, especially
  analogue electronics, Audio over IP and reverse engineering embedded devices.
</p>

<h2 id="aveiro">Aveiro, research and Linux</h2>

<p>
  While I was still doing my bachelor's, I started working at Instituto de Telecomunicações in 2023
  through an undergraduate research grant. My work there continued until 2026, taking me from
  exploring IPFS in 5G scenarios for edge computing to the Aveiro Tech City Living Lab (ATCLL),
  where I developed a platform for viewing data from the city's LiDAR, radar and cameras in real
  time. I later extended it to look back through historical data, including average speeds along
  road sections, congestion and parking statistics. Along the way there was plenty of code to write,
  documentation to read and decisions to document.
</p>
<p>
  Outside the research work, I spent a lot of time involved with Linux. I started with Ubuntu and
  never really wanted to go back to Windows, partly because I like GNOME's design and partly because
  of how much I can automate from the command line. With Linux servers in my homelab too, it makes
  sense to get to know that environment well. One of the things I value most about open source is
  being able to read someone else's code, work out how they approached a problem and learn from it.
</p>
<p>
  At university, that interest also gave me a way to meet people through GLUA, the University of
  Aveiro's Linux group, where I was a coordinator. I ran introductory Linux workshops, wrote
  installation and troubleshooting guides, and made a script to install Quartus Prime Lite on Ubuntu
  and Fedora. GLUA is a big part of why I'm so fond of the university, along with the professors and
  security staff I got to know and the department itself, which I've become rather attached to.
</p>

<h2 id="offline">Away from the desk</h2>

<p>
  Back in Estarreja, a lot of that social side of my life happens around the folk group where I play
  accordion and the carnival group where I help with whatever needs doing, from mechanical work to
  costumes. I also make liqueurs and take on quite a few odd jobs: repairing computers, doing
  electrical installations, hiring out and setting up sound for corporate events, working behind a
  bar or even building OSB walls. Friends and colleagues often come to me when they need an idea for
  solving something.
</p>
<p>
  Running has been part of my life since I was little, although it's taken a back seat over the last
  few years. I still enjoy getting out for a walk, and a good weekend has room for dinner with
  friends, a rehearsal with the folk group or time with my girlfriend. I'm a sociable person and
  need to feel comfortable with the people around me, which is something I look for in a team at
  work too: a welcoming place where I can ask questions freely, because I'll probably have quite a
  few.
</p>
